WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.970
YUFENG GUO: What if
you could inspect

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:05.370
a machine learning model
with minimal code required?

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.980
What if you could even see
if your model was being fair

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.060
or not?

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.820
We'll cover all that and
more on today's episode

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.942
of "AI Adventures."

00:00:13.942 --> 00:00:17.379
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.710
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:27.620
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:29.960
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:31.420
we're going to
look at the What-If

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.720
Tool, an open-source project
from the People and AI Research

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.250
team at Google.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:39.550
It's designed to help you
probe your models to better

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:41.600
understand its behavior.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.360
Now, a few episodes
ago, we looked

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.550
at Lucid, which was
great for understanding

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:47.440
convolutional networks.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:48.880
It's only fitting
that we now look

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.580
at What-If Tool, which
specializes in structured data

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:52.903
and text.

00:00:52.903 --> 00:00:54.570
Let's take a look at
what it looks like.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:58.630
The What-If Tool, or WIT, can be
used inside a Jupyter or Colab

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:03.520
notebook or embedded into the
TensorBoard web application.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.230
Today, we'll just
look at one example

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:08.320
data set, our old friend,
the US Census data set.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.320
It's a nicely-sized data set
for this type of exploration

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:14.900
and it has many of the flaws
of a real-world data set,

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:17.710
so we can have some pretty
interesting findings.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.780
In this example, two
models were trained

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.000
on the Census data set,
a linear classifier

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.330
and a deep classifier.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:26.260
So they'll be compared to
each other as we use the tool.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:27.850
Within the tool,
they are referred

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:30.280
to as Model 1 and Model 2.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:31.870
The first view of
the What-If Tool

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:34.870
has Facets Dive embedded in it.

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:37.540
You can use it to slice and
dice your data along the x

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.360
and y-axes, as well
as see how your data

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:41.980
points are distributed.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.740
Longtime viewers will
recall an early episode

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:47.350
of "AI Adventures"
about the Facets tool.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:49.600
If you were wondering whatever
happened to that, well,

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:50.433
here's your answer.

00:01:50.433 --> 00:01:52.600
The Facets tool has been
integrated into the What-If

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.330
Tool and updated
with more features.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:57.220
Facets Dive is
incredibly flexible

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.780
and can create multiple
interesting visualizations

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:03.850
through its ability to bucket,
scatter, and color data points.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:05.800
You can click on
values in the chart

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:08.889
and edit them to see
what the prediction would

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:11.950
be for your newly edited value.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:14.290
Try doing this near
the decision boundary

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:17.260
to get a better understanding
of what features have a greater

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.800
effect on the outcome
of your predictions.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.130
Here, we change the age and
hours per week of a data point

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.560
to see if that changes the
outcome of the prediction

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:27.940
and by how much.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:30.520
Notice that the predictions
for both the linear model

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.690
and the deep model
are shown here.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:34.390
This view also
features an ability

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:37.390
to show the nearest
counterfactuals.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.270
The nearest counterfactual is
the most similar data point

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:42.860
that got a different
classification.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.830
Once you turn on the
toggle, each time

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:47.080
you click a data point,
its counterfactual

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:48.340
also gets highlighted.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.410
Here, we see an example
where these two data

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:54.340
points are very similar, but
the occupations are different.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.470
Now let's move over
to the next tab.

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:59.570
Yeah, this tool's got two
more tabs of information.

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:02.320
The next one is called
Performance and Fairness,

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.900
which allows us to look at
overall model performance

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:09.400
and ask questions about model
performance across data slices.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.750
Be sure to set that
Ground Truth feature

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.750
in the upper left, which
is the label that we'll

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:17.410
be predicting on, in order to
get the most out of this panel.

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:19.780
We can try moving the
prediction threshold around

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:22.790
for each of the models to see
how it impacts that confusion

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:23.500
matrix.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.440
We can also choose to
slice by up to two fields

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.800
and then select various
fairness optimization strategies

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:34.240
to see how that affects the
threshold values in each model.

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:36.670
Now, the third tab is
not to be left out.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:40.810
Here, we see Facets Overview
embedded into the What-If Tool.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:43.600
You can use this to look at
the distribution of your data

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.210
points across all the
different features,

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:49.570
as well as some key
information about each feature.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:52.940
This is a great way to spot
imbalanced data, as well.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:56.020
There's a lot of features, a
lot of use cases for the What-If

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:59.420
Tool, so we're not going to be
able to cover them all today.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:02.090
But if you're interested
in learning more about it,

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:04.270
let me know in the comments
below or on Twitter,

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:06.160
and I'll try to make
another episode to dive

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:09.560
into the other features of
this tool in more detail.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.220
And if you can't wait for that
to happen, go ahead and join

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:15.310
the What-If Tool community
on our Google Group.

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:17.470
There, you'll find
announcements of new features

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:19.540
and all sorts of
lively discussions.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:22.990
I've included a clickable
link below in the description.

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:26.080
Thanks for watching this episode
of Cloud "AI Adventures."

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.060
And if you enjoyed
it, click the button

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:31.580
and be sure to subscribe to get
all the latest episodes right

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:32.780
when they come out.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:34.480
For now, check out
the What-If Tool

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.580
and learn about how
your model is operating.

